From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eugene Feinberg <uge@persimmons.ai>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 14:30:40 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 10/37] LLHD UnrollLoops: also unroll inside llhd.process, not
 just llhd.combinational

The pass only ever visited CombinationalOp, so a `for` loop in a CLOCKED
process stayed folded. That has a real downstream cost rather than being
merely a missed optimisation: Deseq cannot see through the loop to the
drive's enable, so the enable stays an opaque unknown that appears as a
common factor on EVERY term of the value table -- including the reset term,
which matchDriveClockAndReset has no enable variant for (the clock has
clockWithEnable; the reset has no counterpart). The drive is rejected as
'unknown reset scheme', the process survives to LowerProcesses, and under
assume-complete-sensitivity it was then lowered as combinational and its
state destroyed (see e4713e808).

So this fixes the cause rather than loosening the reset matcher, which would
have meant accepting an enable on an ASYNC RESET path -- a stronger reset
than the RTL asks for, and the last place to guess.

The process's own wait-loop is not at risk: its back edge is an llhd.wait,
not a cf.br, so it never presents the unique-latch constant-trip-count shape
unrollLoops requires and is skipped like any other unanalyzable loop.

Ordering matters and is not optional: --llhd-unroll-loops must run BEFORE
--llhd-deseq to have this effect. With the old order (deseq first) OpenC910
is unchanged at 4,782 registers / 38 residual llhd ops.

OpenC910, unroll-before-deseq:
  registers            4,782 -> 4,787   (5 recovered, previously destroyed)
  residual llhd ops       38 -> 11
  modules holding llhd     3 -> 1       (ct_ebiu_write_channel, whose loop
                                         still does not unroll)
CVA6 cv32a60x: 263 -> 264 registers, 389 -> 383 llhd ops, 21 -> 20 modules.

ibex unaffected: core.mlir, core_deagg.mlir, struct.mlir byte-identical,
netlist unchanged. Its own lowering script runs deseq before unroll, so the new
ordering does not reach it.
---
 lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/UnrollLoops.cpp | 33 +++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/UnrollLoops.cpp b/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/UnrollLoops.cpp
index 1af3f5691..94e951bfc 100644
--- a/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/UnrollLoops.cpp
+++ b/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/UnrollLoops.cpp
@@ -421,34 +421,49 @@ namespace {
 struct UnrollLoopsPass
     : public llhd::impl::UnrollLoopsPassBase<UnrollLoopsPass> {
   void runOnOperation() override;
-  void runOnOperation(CombinationalOp op);
-  bool unrollLoops(CombinationalOp op);
+  void runOnOperation(Operation *op, Region &body);
+  bool unrollLoops(Operation *op, Region &body);
 };
 } // namespace
 
 void UnrollLoopsPass::runOnOperation() {
   for (auto op : getOperation().getOps<CombinationalOp>())
-    runOnOperation(op);
+    runOnOperation(op, op.getBody());
+
+  // Also unroll inside `llhd.process`. A `for` loop in a CLOCKED process is
+  // otherwise left folded, and that has a real downstream cost: `Deseq`
+  // cannot see through the loop to the drive's enable, so the enable stays an
+  // opaque unknown that appears as a common factor on every term of the value
+  // table -- including the reset term, which `matchDriveClockAndReset` has no
+  // enable variant for. The drive is then rejected as an "unknown reset
+  // scheme" and the process survives to `LowerProcesses`.
+  //
+  // The process's own wait-loop is not at risk here: its back edge is an
+  // `llhd.wait`, not a `cf.br`, so it never presents the unique-latch,
+  // constant-trip-count shape `unrollLoops` requires, and is skipped like any
+  // other unanalyzable loop.
+  for (auto op : getOperation().getOps<ProcessOp>())
+    runOnOperation(op, op.getBody());
 }
 
-void UnrollLoopsPass::runOnOperation(CombinationalOp op) {
+void UnrollLoopsPass::runOnOperation(Operation *op, Region &body) {
   // Unrolling a loop may open up opportunities to unroll its nested loops.
   // Iterate until all possible loops are unrolled or the limit is reached.
   for (unsigned i = 0; i < 16; ++i)
-    if (!unrollLoops(op))
+    if (!unrollLoops(op, body))
       break;
 }
 
-bool UnrollLoopsPass::unrollLoops(CombinationalOp op) {
+bool UnrollLoopsPass::unrollLoops(Operation *op, Region &body) {
   // There's nothing to do if we only have a single block. MLIR even refuses to
   // compute a dominator tree in that case.
-  if (op.getBody().hasOneBlock())
+  if (body.hasOneBlock())
     return false;
 
   // Find the loops.
-  LLVM_DEBUG(llvm::dbgs() << "Unrolling loops in " << op.getLoc() << "\n");
+  LLVM_DEBUG(llvm::dbgs() << "Unrolling loops in " << op->getLoc() << "\n");
   DominanceInfo domInfo(op);
-  CFGLoopInfo cfgLoopInfo(domInfo.getDomTree(&op.getBody()));
+  CFGLoopInfo cfgLoopInfo(domInfo.getDomTree(&body));
 
   // We only support simple loops where there is a single back-edge to the
   // header, and the latch block has a back-edge to a single header. Create a
